Topi Linkala <n...@iki.fi> wrote:
> sjdevn...@yahoo.com wrote:
>
> > I agree for the most part, but why name Sting?  Except against orcs
> > (and spider webs) it's just an elven dagger, and you are making future
> > sacs/wishes for artifacts harder.

Part of the reason is to teach myself to avoid melee combat
while paying a wizard.  Part is orcs are quite common in the
early game so being warned about them is a significant
advantage.  Sting is wooden so it can be used against acid
monsters but that applies against iron weapons not the
quarterstaff so it only applies to non-wizards.  Part of the
reason is to have a wielded dagger to help #enhance to
Expert on the off chance that I can't avoid melee combat as
I run for the stairs.  If I'm going to wield a dagger, may as well
be an artifact one.

> First sacrifice gift is not affected and wizard does not need more [1].
> On the other hand orcs are plenty so to see them from the other half of
> the map is a great boon.

And double damage against orcs doesn't suck in the early
game even if it doesn't apply to other monsters.

In general for classes that can get to Expert at dagger I name
Sting early.  It's a tactic I decided on a while ago and so far
I don't regret adopting it.

> [1] If a chaotic wizard wants the Stormbringer more the reason to name
> both Sting and Orcrist so that you don't get them from the sacrifice.

A chaotic elven non-wizard can be guaranteed Stormy doing
that.  The first gift is always co-aligned so it doesn't include
the Brands and it is racial so an elven chaotic never gets
Grimtooth first gift.  For that to apply to a wizard would take
a wish for Magicbane - I haven't gotten a wish before the first
gift in a very long time as I rarely do fountains except for
Excalibur any more, but I would sure use it on Magicbane
hoping to switch to Stormy if an early wish came up.

I'll altar dance with chaotic wizards trying for Stormy before
switching to prayer for books.  If I happen to get one of
the other super artifacts as my melee backup isn't of
Stormy, that doesn't suck either.

> The chance of getting a sacrifice gift is inversly proportional to the
> product of existing artifacts and given gifts[2]. Now for the first gift
> the number of existing artifacts doesn't mean a thing, but further more
> it does. If Sting and Orcrist are named then there are three artifacts
> and one gift, but if they are given as gifts there are three artifacts
> and three gifts.
>
> [2] Plus some constant[3] so that when the product is zero we don't
> divide with zero.

This is why the first gift does not take existing non-gift artifacts
into account.  That includes named and wished ones.



